WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.390 --> 00:00:06.999
bjbj, TEASER DEIS - $600,000 to $1 million
we ve committed to for each employee and we

00:00:06.999 --> 00:00:09.680
ve set aside nothing. TITLE CARD - As Goes
Stockton Goes California? INTRO [broll - downtown

00:00:09.680 --> 00:00:13.820
Stockton sign, headlines, city council broll]
In late June 2012, Stockton, California filed

00:00:13.820 --> 00:00:21.960
Chapter Nine bankruptcy. It wasn t the first
California city to do so, and with San Bernardino

00:00:21.960 --> 00:00:27.699
following suit weeks later, it s not the last.
But so far, with a population of just under

00:00:27.699 --> 00:00:37.840
300,000, it is the biggest in U.S. history.
[broll - Stockton map, San Francisco, Stockton

00:00:37.840 --> 00:00:45.219
home values graph] An agricultural center
located just outside of San Francisco in California

00:00:45.219 --> 00:00:50.780
s Central Valley, Stockton was a booming suburb
in the 1990s, providing an affordable living

00:00:50.780 --> 00:00:54.780
alternative to the pricey real estate of the
Bay Area. [broll - public works projects]

00:00:54.780 --> 00:00:59.309
Living large off the new property taxes created
by the housing bubble, the city government

00:00:59.309 --> 00:01:05.420
spent lavishly on big public works projects,
like a waterfront park, an arena, and a brand

00:01:05.420 --> 00:01:10.820
new city hall, which sits nearly empty as
the city government could never actually afford

00:01:10.820 --> 00:01:11.820
to move in. For awhile, things seemed to be
looking up for Stockton, which has long been

00:01:11.820 --> 00:01:14.380
one of California s most economically depressed
cities. Promotional flyers still remain plastered

00:01:14.380 --> 00:01:19.610
to empty buildings downtown, reminders of
a more hopeful time. The city council also

00:01:19.610 --> 00:01:24.390
caved to increasing pressure from the city
s public employee unions and approved major

00:01:24.390 --> 00:01:25.390
bumps in their benefit packages, including
lifetime medical benefits to city retirees

00:01:25.390 --> 00:01:28.810
with as little as one month employment in
the city. The city now faces more than $800

00:01:28.810 --> 00:01:33.280
million dollars in unfunded liabilities for
pensions and other post-employment benefits.

00:01:33.280 --> 00:01:36.530
MILLER - 12:20 - our revenues plummeted. So
we're saddled with this debt that we don't

00:01:36.530 --> 00:01:42.200
have the revenues anymore to service. With
revenue dwindling in the wake of the housing

00:01:42.200 --> 00:01:48.659
crash, Stockton issued a $125 million bond
in 2007 to help fill the budget gap. But these

00:01:48.659 --> 00:01:54.880
back-end loaded bonds saddled the city with
an annual payment of $16.8 million as of this

00:01:54.880 --> 00:02:00.869
year, with ballooning payments rising to $22.3
million next year, and a nearly doubling to

00:02:00.869 --> 00:02:01.869
$30.2 million by 2020. City council member
and Vice Mayor Kathy Miller was elected in

00:02:01.869 --> 00:02:03.560
2009 and says that while she was expecting
to clean up a mess when she got into office,

00:02:03.560 --> 00:02:05.259
she didn t realize it would be this big of
a mess. MILLER - 1:40 - I was shocked by how

00:02:05.259 --> 00:02:07.689
deep it went, by how ingrained it was, by
how long bad decisions had been made in the

00:02:07.689 --> 00:02:10.850
city, and how it had really created a culture
that was not responsive to the public. 2:10

00:02:10.850 --> 00:02:15.230
- It was internally focused on what was best
for the city and what was best for its employees,

00:02:15.230 --> 00:02:21.989
not necessarily what was best for the public.
Miller describes a political culture in which

00:02:21.989 --> 00:02:26.680
generous handouts to public employee unions
were masked through the tricky accounting

00:02:26.680 --> 00:02:31.459
of a savvy city manager who enabled local
government officials to give in to nearly

00:02:31.459 --> 00:02:35.769
every union demand. MILLER - the quality of
that information that the councils received

00:02:35.769 --> 00:02:43.959
in the nineties, from then city manager Dwayne
Milnes, was not complete. It was not always

00:02:43.959 --> 00:02:53.831
accurate. And in some cases, it was almost
misleading. Dwayne Milnes was Stockton s city

00:02:53.831 --> 00:02:58.069
manager in the 90s and signed off on many
of these benefit packages. MILNES - 20:20

00:02:58.069 --> 00:03:03.730
- in retrospect, we should have been more
restrictive in that plan than we were. I say,

00:03:03.730 --> 00:03:04.730
"in retrospect" because when you look at what's
happened with medical inflation, we really

00:03:04.730 --> 00:03:08.380
should have. But we didn't know it was going
to happen. Milnes now runs Stockton s Association

00:03:08.380 --> 00:03:12.940
of Retired Employees, which is fighting to
preserve many of those very same benefits

00:03:12.940 --> 00:03:14.986
that Milnes now admits were a mistake for
the city, like a medical plan offering free

00:03:14.986 --> 00:03:15.986
lifetime care to workers at a cost of $417
million over the next ten years. MILNES - :26

00:03:15.986 --> 00:03:16.986
- The city unilaterally reduced the medical
benefits of retirees by about 30% without

00:03:16.986 --> 00:03:17.986
notice, and it was important that we form
an association so that we could approach the

00:03:17.986 --> 00:03:19.200
city and say, "look, these are vested rights.
If you have a need to reduce them, you need

00:03:19.200 --> 00:03:23.909
to come talk to us." Milnes doesn t believe
the he or the city council members he advised

00:03:23.909 --> 00:03:29.000
are to blame for Stockton s dire situation
and says that the economic downturn would

00:03:29.000 --> 00:03:32.790
have put Stockton in this situation with or
without the public spending binge. MILNES

00:03:32.790 --> 00:03:37.850
- 10:00 - I've seen cities that have this
same pension system paying pretty much the

00:03:37.850 --> 00:03:44.900
same pension rates that Stockton is doing
and are not about ready to fall off the cliff.

00:03:44.900 --> 00:03:48.360
They're making adjustments. 10:27 - When you
ask who's going to tip over, it's not who

00:03:48.360 --> 00:03:53.159
is providing the pension benefits. It's who
is providing the pension benefits in an economic

00:03:53.159 --> 00:03:58.610
environment where they can't afford it. But
Reason Foundation Senior Policy Analyst Adam

00:03:58.610 --> 00:04:04.750
Summers says that Stockton s story is becoming
an increasingly common one, as public officials

00:04:04.750 --> 00:04:09.832
have no incentive to rein in spending before
hitting the brink. ADAM SUMMERS You re going

00:04:09.832 --> 00:04:14.459
to see more and more cities kind of going
the way of Stockton and San Bernardino and

00:04:14.459 --> 00:04:27.340
Vallejo . You have moral hazard won t have
to bear the costs of those decisions. 30:30

00:04:27.340 --> 00:04:30.782
- we had a City Manager who publicly has stated
all during the nineties while he was here,

00:04:30.782 --> 00:04:36.390
he didn't like having to negotiate every year
with his public employee unions, and so, he

00:04:36.390 --> 00:04:44.260
granted generous long-term contracts, because
he didn't want to do it. 26:55 - Malarkey.

00:04:44.260 --> 00:04:52.800
She was not in the closed sessions when we
were very, very clear with the city council

00:04:52.800 --> 00:04:57.940
in detail, what was being negotiated, what
the cost of those things were. I'm the last

00:04:57.940 --> 00:05:04.360
guy who's going to tell you what you want
to hear. Putting the history for the bad deal

00:05:04.360 --> 00:05:14.240
aside, today s reality is that the unfunded
retiree benefits have gutted the city s general

00:05:14.240 --> 00:05:23.290
fund, though it s supposed to pay for the
most basic government services like police

00:05:23.290 --> 00:05:28.900
and fire. ADAM SUMMERS When you have govt.
making promises it can t keep, it s eating

00:05:28.900 --> 00:05:34.810
up resources for public safety . And that
has a kind of ripple effect on the general

00:05:34.810 --> 00:05:39.130
fund for everything the government does. One
result in Stockton is that the police force

00:05:39.130 --> 00:05:46.690
shrank by 26 percent in four years, and last
year the city faced a record 58 murders. Halfway

00:05:46.690 --> 00:05:52.050
into 2012, they ve already had 34. NANCE - 5:00
- Stockton's a very dangerous place to live.

00:05:52.050 --> 00:05:56.880
5:30 - Anybody can be victimized. The number
of car robberies is going up, the number of

00:05:56.880 --> 00:06:00.271
shootings, it's all rising. So people are
more likely to be a victim of a crime then

00:06:00.271 --> 00:06:04.600
they ever have in their life. Kathryn Nance
is a police sergeant and member of the Stockton

00:06:04.600 --> 00:06:09.020
Police Officers Association, which has several
lawsuits against the city. NANCE - 6:50 - They

00:06:09.020 --> 00:06:10.738
kept spending in a lot of different places,
they kept spending with labor unions, kept

00:06:10.738 --> 00:06:11.738
spending on contracts, but kept spending to
build things they couldn't afford. So while

00:06:11.738 --> 00:06:12.738
the police officers and the fire dpeartment
at fair market value at one point, now that's

00:06:12.738 --> 00:06:15.920
taken away because we're now below market
value and unable to retain or recruit people.

00:06:15.920 --> 00:06:20.520
But in yet another example of the city s loose
accounting, 3 of the last 4 police chiefs

00:06:20.520 --> 00:06:25.700
served fewer than three years in Stockton,
and used the inflated salaries in those final

00:06:25.700 --> 00:06:31.830
years of their service to retire with massive
pensions, some of which exceed $200,000 a

00:06:31.830 --> 00:06:36.580
year. While the last remnants of a fading
movement Occupy one of the city s nearly empty

00:06:36.580 --> 00:06:42.340
public parks, Kathy Miller says it s not corporate
pay, but public sector pay that s out of sync

00:06:42.340 --> 00:06:44.570
with reality in Stockton. MILLER - 8:00 - the
compensation for public employees, the gap

00:06:44.570 --> 00:06:51.070
widened between what they were earning and
what the average taxpayer was earning. I mean,

00:06:51.070 --> 00:06:56.310
this is in a county where the average living
person earns less than fifty thousand dollars

00:06:56.310 --> 00:06:58.010
a year. MILLER 8:15 - I think it created a
culture where public employees, there's a

00:06:58.010 --> 00:07:00.200
real disconnect between them and the average
working family in our county. While California

00:07:00.200 --> 00:07:06.800
s state-run pension system, CalPers, has left
cities particularly vulnerable to budget meltdowns,

00:07:06.800 --> 00:07:11.030
Summers warns that the problem isn t confined
to the Golden State. ADAM SUMMERS Cities across

00:07:11.030 --> 00:07:23.100
the country have made the same poor decisions
stress on local budgets SUMMERS I think they

00:07:23.100 --> 00:07:36.350
re going to have to follow the example of
San Diego 47:58 - this 

00:07:36.350 --> 00:07:47.040
is 
not a partisan issue at all. This is a good

00:07:47.040 --> 00:07:56.090
government issue. when we get out of touch
with that and begin acting like it's our money,

00:07:56.090 --> 00:08:00.901
it's not the people's money, that's where
you get into real serious problems. Stockton

00:08:00.901 --> 00:08:06.440
officials hope bankruptcy will allow the city
to opt out of some of its more stifling contracts

00:08:06.440 --> 00:08:11.710
and to avoid repaying all of the bondholders
while maintaining some money in the general

00:08:11.710 --> 00:08:16.970
fund to keep its most basic city services.
The city has already been downgraded by credit

00:08:16.970 --> 00:08:22.330
ratings agencies, which will severely restrict
its borrowing ability. Retirees probably won

00:08:22.330 --> 00:08:26.750
t be seeing the benefits they were promised
by city officials. And taxpayers aren t likely

00:08:26.750 --> 00:08:31.490
to see improved city services any time soon.
But with cities across the country locked

00:08:31.490 --> 00:08:38.050
into expensive long-term contracts, Stockton
s title as the largest U.S. city to ever go

00:08:38.050 --> 00:08:40.104
bankrupt might be short-lived. gdeL nbnbnbnbnR
